ormat="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Railways acted without schedules or personnel</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Bureaucracy prevented deportation</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer></question><question type="multichoice"><name><text>WH_U05_L06_Q16 A01 Cause and effect</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> WH_U05_L06_Q16</p><div style="border:3px solid #7f1d1d;background:#fff1f2;padding:10px;text-align:center"><h3>Persecution escalation model</h3><p><b>Antisemitic ideology and prejudice</b> → propaganda → legal exclusion → economic/social persecution → forced concentration and deportation → mass murder</p><p style="background:#fef3c7;padding:7px"><b>This sequence was produced through human decisions, institutions, and wartime escalation; it was not inevitable.</b></p></div><p>Which explanation best avoids inevitability?</p>]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review Unit 5 Lesson 6 evidence and reasoning.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback><defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><hidden>0</hidden><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>ABCD</answernumbering><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Antisemitic ideology enabled persecution, but laws, war, institutions, collaboration, and repeated human decisions drove escalation</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Correct.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Prejudice automatically produces genocide in every society</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>One law alone completed the Holocaust</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>No individual or institution made choices</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer></question><question type="multichoice"><name><text>WH_U05_L06_Q17 A02 Chronology</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> WH_U05_L06_Q17</p><table border="1" cellpadding="6" style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%"><caption><strong>Escalation of Nazi persecution and mass murder</strong></caption><tr style="background:#fee2e2"><th>Date</th><th>Development</th></tr><tr><td>1933</td><td>Early exclusion, arrests, boycotts, and persecution</td></tr><tr><td>1935</td><td>Nuremberg Laws institutionalize racial exclusion</td></tr><tr><td>Nov. 1938</td><td>Kristallnacht: coordinated anti-Jewish violence</td></tr><tr><td>1939–40</td><td>Occupation and forced confinement in ghettos</td></tr><tr><td>1941</td><td>Einsatzgruppen mass shootings expand after invasion of Soviet Union</td></tr><tr><td>1942</td><td>Deportations and killing-center operations expand</td></tr><tr><td>1943</td><td>Warsaw Ghetto Uprising and other resistance</td></tr><tr><td>1944–45</td><td>Continued deportations; camps liberated as Nazi Germany collapses</td></tr></table><p>Why did war beginning in 1939 matter to escalation?</p>]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review Unit 5 Lesson 6 evidence and reasoning.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback><defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><hidden>0</hidden><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>ABCD</answernumbering><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Occupation placed millions more Jews under Nazi control and enabled ghettos, shootings, deportations, and killing operations</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Correct.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>War ended Nazi authority</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Occupation restored equal rights</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Mass shootings ended before invasion</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer></question><question type="multichoice"><name><text>WH_U05_L06_Q18 A03 CER</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> WH_U05_L06_Q18</p><blockquote style="border-left:5px solid #991b1b;padding:9px;background:#fff1f2"><p>“A citizen of the Reich is only that subject who is of German or kindred blood...”</p><footer>Reich Citizenship Law, September 15, 1935; translated excerpt in the USHMM document collection.</footer></blockquote><p>Which CER statement is strongest?</p>]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review Unit 5 Lesson 6 evidence and reasoning.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback><defaultgrade>1</defaultgrade><penalty>0.3333333</penalty><hidden>0</hidden><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answernumbering>ABCD</answernumbering><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Claim: Nazi persecution became state policy. Evidence: the law restricted citizenship by ancestry. Reasoning: government authority institutionalized racist exclusion and enabled further persecution.</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Correct.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Claim: Equality expanded. Evidence: rights narrowed. Reasoning: exclusion is inclusion.</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Claim: The law caused every later action alone. Evidence: one sentence. Reasoning: no other decision mattered.</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Claim: The law concerned railways. Evidence: blood. Reasoning: transport only.</p>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review the evidence.</p>]]></text></feedback></answer></question><question type="multichoice"><name><text>WH_U05_L06_Q19 A04 Human choices</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Question ID:</strong> WH_U05_L06_Q19</p><table border="1" cellpadding="6" style="border-collapse:collapse;width:100%"><caption><strong>Human choices framework</strong></caption><tr style="background:#dcfce7"><th>Role</th><th>Possible documented actions</th></tr><tr><td>Perpetrator/collaborator</td><td>Planning, arrest, confiscation, deportation, guarding, killing, or assisting persecution</td></tr><tr><td>Bystander</td><td>Witnessing or knowing without directly participating; circumstances and options varied</td></tr><tr><td>Rescuer/resister</td><td>Hiding people, forging documents, aiding escape, documenting crimes, armed or nonviolent opposition</td></tr><tr><td>Victim/survivor</td><td>Survival strategies, documentation, mutual aid, spiritual, cultural, nonviolent, or armed resistance</td></tr></table><p><small>Labels require evidence; individuals' circumstances and actions could be complex.</small></p><p>Which conclusion is most responsible?</p>]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Review Unit 5 Lesson 6 evidence and reasoning.</p>]]></text></generalfeedback><defaultgr